VIG = "Mrs S, a 54-year-old school teacher from Chennai with type 2 diabetes for 11 years, takes metformin 1 g twice daily and glimepiride 4 mg daily. She has lost 4 kg in 3 months, has nocturia, and her HbA1c is 10.8% with fasting glucose 238 mg/dL. Weight is 68 kg, eGFR 72 mL/min/1.73 m². She is afraid that insulin means 'the last stage' and that injections will hurt."

EXP = [
 explain("pathophysiology", "Why most people with type 2 diabetes eventually need insulin", "Is needing insulin a failure?",
  "Type 2 diabetes is a progressive loss of beta-cell function on a background of insulin resistance. Oral drugs and lifestyle changes work while enough beta-cell function remains, but over a decade many people — and in India, often earlier and at a lower body weight — no longer make enough insulin. Weight loss, rising glucose despite good adherence and catabolic features are signs of insulin deficiency, not of poor effort. Basal insulin replaces the background insulin that suppresses glucose production from the liver overnight and between meals, which is why it lowers fasting glucose so effectively. Prandial insulin covers the glucose rise after meals. Explaining this removes the stigma that insulin is a punishment or the 'last stage'."),
 explain("signs-symptoms", "Recognising when insulin is needed — and when it is too much", "Which features tell you to start, and which tell you to stop escalating?",
  "Start insulin when HbA1c is above 10%, glucose is 300 mg/dL or more, or the patient has symptoms such as weight loss, polyuria and thirst (ADA 2025; ICMR uses HbA1c above 10%). Look for type 1 diabetes or LADA in lean adults whose oral drugs fail early, and for fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes in young lean patients with abdominal pain. Once on insulin, watch for overbasalisation: a basal dose above about 0.5 units/kg, a fall of 50 mg/dL or more from bedtime to morning, hypoglycaemia, or widely swinging readings. These mean the patient needs prandial cover, not more basal insulin. Night sweats, nightmares and morning headaches suggest nocturnal hypoglycaemia; a check between midnight and 2 am confirms it."),
 explain("investigations", "Monitoring that makes titration safe", "What does the patient need to measure?",
  "Self-monitoring of blood glucose is what makes insulin safe. During basal titration the key reading is the fasting glucose each morning; when prandial insulin is added, readings 2 hours after meals guide those doses. People with type 1 diabetes test before each meal and at bedtime, with an occasional check between midnight and 2 am. HbA1c every 3 months shows whether the overall plan is working. Check ketones when glucose is above 250 mg/dL during illness. Annual creatinine and eGFR matter because insulin needs fall as kidney function declines, and the injection sites should be examined at every visit for lipohypertrophy, which causes erratic absorption."),
 explain("management", "A stepwise insulin plan", "How do you start, titrate and intensify?",
  "Keep metformin and other non-insulin drugs unless contraindicated, and reduce or stop the sulfonylurea when prandial insulin is added. Start basal insulin at 0.1–0.2 units/kg at bedtime — NPH is cheaper, glargine causes less nocturnal hypoglycaemia. Teach the patient to raise the dose by 2 units every 3 days until fasting glucose reaches 80–130 mg/dL, and to lower it after any hypoglycaemia. When fasting glucose is at target but HbA1c is still high, or the basal dose exceeds about 0.5 units/kg, add 4 units (or 10% of the basal dose) of prandial insulin before the largest meal, or switch to premixed insulin twice daily. Every patient on insulin needs a hypoglycaemia plan: 3 teaspoons of glucose for mild episodes, glucagon 1 mg for severe ones, and sick-day rules.")]

QS = [
 bq("q1", "A 54-year-old school teacher with type 2 diabetes for 11 years takes metformin 1 g twice daily and glimepiride 4 mg daily with good adherence. HbA1c is 10.8%, fasting glucose 238 mg/dL, and she has lost 4 kg in 3 months with nocturia. Weight is 68 kg and eGFR 72 mL/min/1.73 m².", "What is the best next step?",
  [("Add a third oral drug and review in 6 months", "Weight loss with HbA1c above 10% indicates insulin deficiency; insulin is recommended (ADA 2025; ICMR)."),
   ("Start basal insulin about 7–14 units at bedtime and continue metformin", "Correct. 0.1–0.2 U/kg for 68 kg is about 7–14 units; keep metformin (ADA 2025)."),
   ("Stop all oral drugs and start basal-bolus insulin 1 U/kg/day", "Too much, too fast: basal insulin is started first and metformin continued."),
   ("Start premixed insulin 30 units twice daily", "An unnecessarily high starting dose with high hypoglycaemia risk."),
   ("Increase glimepiride to 8 mg", "Higher sulfonylurea doses add little and raise hypoglycaemia risk when beta-cell function is failing.")],
  "B", "Recognise the indication for insulin and choose a safe starting dose.", "Start basal 0.1–0.2 U/kg at bedtime; keep metformin."),
 bq("q2", "A 60-year-old man on glargine has titrated to 52 units daily (weight 80 kg). Fasting glucose is 110–125 mg/dL, but HbA1c is 8.9% and he has had two episodes of hypoglycaemia at 3 am. Bedtime glucose is around 220 mg/dL.", "What is the most appropriate change?",
  [("Increase glargine by 4 units", "He is overbasalised; more basal insulin increases nocturnal hypoglycaemia."),
   ("Switch glargine to NPH at the same dose", "NPH increases nocturnal hypoglycaemia risk and does not address post-meal glucose."),
   ("Add glimepiride", "A sulfonylurea adds hypoglycaemia risk and does not target the problem."),
   ("Reduce glargine and add prandial insulin before the largest meal", "Correct. Basal above 0.5 U/kg (52 units for 80 kg = 0.65 U/kg), a large bedtime-to-morning fall and nocturnal hypoglycaemia signal overbasalisation; add prandial cover (ADA guide)."),
   ("No change because fasting glucose is at target", "HbA1c above goal with nocturnal hypoglycaemia needs a change.")],
  "D", "Recognise overbasalisation and intensify with prandial insulin.", "Basal above 0.5 U/kg with hypoglycaemia or large overnight fall: add prandial insulin, not more basal."),
 bq("q3", "A 19-year-old college student with type 1 diabetes on basal-bolus insulin (total 48 units a day) phones the clinic. He has fever and has vomited twice since morning. His glucose is 310 mg/dL and urine ketones are moderate. He can still drink fluids and asks whether he should skip insulin because he cannot eat.", "What is the correct advice?",
  [("Stop all insulin until he can eat", "Stopping insulin precipitates diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA); insulin is never stopped on sick days (ICMR T1DM)."),
   ("Continue usual insulin and add regular insulin 10–15% of the total daily dose every 6 hours, with fluids and glucose checks; come in if vomiting persists or ketones rise", "Correct. These are the ICMR T1DM sick-day rules with admission criteria."),
   ("Take only basal insulin and no extra", "Basal alone is not enough; he needs extra insulin to clear the ketones."),
   ("Halve all insulin doses", "Illness raises insulin needs; halving doses risks DKA."),
   ("Take oral glucose only", "His glucose is already high; he needs insulin, fluids and close monitoring, not extra glucose.")],
  "B", "Apply sick-day rules in type 1 diabetes.", "Never stop insulin when ill; give extra 10–15% of TDD every 6 hours and admit if not settling.")]

T = deepen(T, [ALG], VIG, EXP, QS)

save("t71_insulin.json", [T])
